Meeting Title: Day-6 of K8s Last Day / Day-11 of Module-5 with Batch-45 (26th Nov 2026, 8AM IST)

SUMMARY:
This was a hands-on Kubernetes session led by Vikas. The session focused on practical implementation rather than theory. Key activities covered were creating a GKE cluster on Google Cloud, setting up Kubernetes namespaces, deploying secrets and config maps, and deploying NGINX.

PROPER NOTES:

Cluster Creation
Created a Kubernetes cluster on Google Cloud (GKE)
Location: Europe region had issues, so adjusted accordingly
Node pool configured with 1 node, standard size, 30GB storage
Cluster creation takes approximately 3 minutes
Namespace
Command used to create a namespace called "kubernetes-workshop"
Context was set so all future work stays within this namespace
Purpose: Isolated environments for dev, pre-prod, prod, experimental, demo, etc.
Secrets (secrets.yml)
A secret named "DB Secret" was created
Type: Opaque (meaning not visible/see-through, used for sensitive data)
Stored two database credentials inside the secret
Config Map
Used to store non-sensitive configuration information
Purpose: Helps one application connect to another using endpoints/URLs
Config map can be described to view its contents
GitHub Repo
All session code available on GitHub under "KATES demo" repository
Students advised to bookmark the repo
NGINX Deployment
After cluster was ready, NGINX deployment was initiated
Students connected to cluster via Cloud Shell
Q AND A FROM SESSION:

Q: What is a Namespace?
A: An isolated environment used to separate workloads like dev, pre-prod, prod, experimental, etc.

Q: What is written in secrets.yml?
A: A secret named "DB Secret" containing two database credentials.

Q: What does "Opaque" type mean in secrets?
A: Opaque means not visible or see-through, indicating sensitive data that is hidden/blocked.

Q: What is a Config Map?
A: A file storing non-sensitive configuration information, such as endpoints or URLs, used to connect applications within Kubernetes.

Q: How do applications connect in Kubernetes?
A: Either through labels and selectors, or through config maps that provide endpoint/URL information.

